# tools/cr/crypto_poly1305_witness.rish -- Poly1305 (RFC 8439) authored in pure Rye: Season G's fourth crypto primitive, Monocypher's one-time
# authenticator, proven against the RFC 8439 §2.5.2 known-answer and byte-for-byte against an independent implementation (Season G - Cryptography).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography - Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-180728_open-season-g-crypto-blake2b.md (the season's opening read names the ChaCha/Poly line as
# the primitives that follow the hash foundation, each its own round) and active-designing/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-
# decision-wave.md (Poly1305 completes the ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD, the named next Season G crux after ChaCha20).
# Kin: crypto/chacha20.rye - crypto/blake2b.rye - crypto/sha512.rye (the first three primitives, GREEN). Monocypher (vendor/monocypher,
# CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity TARGET, never a copied line -- our own arithmetic, studied clean-room from the public RFC (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/poly1305.rye authors Poly1305 from the RFC -- the clamped coefficient r, the polynomial evaluated over the prime field 2^130 − 5
# in five 26-bit limbs, the ×5 fold that reduces each over-130 carry (2^130 ≡ 5), the pad s added mod 2^128 to yield the 16-byte tag. The
# primitive calls no std.crypto. Parity is proven at selftest against two references runnable now: the RFC 8439 §2.5.2 known-answer (the
# tag for "Cryptographic Forum Research Group" under the RFC's one-time key, hex for hex), and Zig's own std.crypto Poly1305 as an
# independent implementation, byte-for-byte across the empty message, a sub-block input, an exact 16-byte block, one byte over a block, the
# RFC's 34-byte length, and a 256-byte multi-block input. The vendored Monocypher source is a held network fetch, so Monocypher-source
# parity is an honest horizon rung. An over-long call refuses before a block is absorbed. Purely local arithmetic -- the key is the RFC's
# public one-time vector, no real secret, no signature (a MAC authenticates, it does not sign with an identity), no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_poly1305_witness.rish

say "crypto-poly1305: Language EN — Season G's fourth primitive: Poly1305 (RFC 8439), Monocypher's one-time authenticator and the tag half of ChaCha20-Poly1305, the direct next stone after the stream cipher toward the Rye-native authenticated-encryption stack. Pure integer arithmetic over the prime field 2^130 − 5 carried in five 26-bit limbs, so every limb product fits one 64-bit word — no big-integer library, genuinely buildable in authored Rye this round, and it carries the published RFC 8439 §2.5.2 known-answer that ties parity to a neutral public reference."
say "crypto-poly1305: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: a polynomial in the 16-byte message blocks with a clamped secret coefficient r, each over-130 carry folded back as ×5 because 2^130 ≡ 5, the secret pad s added mod 2^128 to yield the 16-byte tag, so the same key and message always yield the same authenticator anyone can replay against the RFC."
say "crypto-poly1305: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std.crypto; it is authored from the RFC, bounded input (max_message_bytes), contract asserts on every function, 64-bit products for the 26-bit limb multiplies where the arithmetic genuinely needs 64 bits. Parity holds against the RFC 8439 §2.5.2 known-answer AND Zig's independent std.crypto Poly1305 byte-for-byte across the empty message, a sub-block input, an exact 16-byte block, one byte over a block, the RFC's 34-byte length, and a multi-block input; an over-long call refuses before a block is absorbed. Monocypher-source parity is a named horizon behind the held vendor fetch. Purely local — the key is the RFC's public one-time vector, no real secret, no signature, no network, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/poly1305"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/poly1305.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-poly1305: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/poly1305.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-poly1305: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-poly1305: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-poly1305' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-poly1305: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-poly1305 line"
